RI is connected to broader governance processes through relationships that tie it to other key actors within the LAI framework, illustrating the commission’s integration into the federal information oversight network [Data: Relationships (2, 201)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"CPADS acts as the input and advisory counterpart in classification decisions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CPADS serves as the Permanent Commission for Evaluation of Classified Documents and specializes in assessing documents to determine their secrecy levels and providing input on classification decisions to the CMRI. This creates a two-tiered system where CPADS’ expertise informs the CMRI’s reevaluation decisions, contributing to consistency and accountability in how classifications are applied and potentially declassified. The collaboration between CPADS and CMRI is a core dynamic within the governance framework, ensuring that input from an independent advisory body informs the final determinations on sensitive materials. This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: Entities (217); Relationships (201)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"CGU’s oversight role and its coordination with the Banco Central\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CGU – the Controladoria-Geral da União – is the federal auditing and oversight body responsible for control, audit, and coordination of classified information. It oversees secrecy practices and serves as the designated destination for classified information to be sent for control and verification. The CGU’s mandate positions it as a key controller and overseer in the information-security lifecycle, including coordination among agencies to ensure proper handling of ultrassecreto material. The relationship network also shows a delegation path where CGU delegates ultrassecreto-case responsibilities to the Banco Central, indicating a formal flow of sensitive information handling to a specialized financial authority when needed. 